---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="CSS Animations Guide" filename="articles/cssanim.htm">
  <p><a href={`${base}articles/`} class="back-link">← Back to Articles</a></p>

  <h1>CSS Animations: A Complete Guide</h1>

  <p><em>Published: January 5, 2024</em></p>

  <hr />

  <p>
    CSS animations are one of the most powerful tools in a web developer's arsenal.
    They can bring interfaces to life and create memorable experiences.
  </p>

  <h2>Transitions vs Animations</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Transitions</strong> are simple: they animate between two states when
    a property changes. Perfect for hover effects.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Animations</strong> are more powerful: they can have multiple keyframes,
    run automatically, and loop infinitely.
  </p>

  <h2>Timing Functions</h2>

  <p>The timing function controls the acceleration curve:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>linear</strong> — Constant speed</li>
    <li><strong>ease</strong> — Slow start, fast middle, slow end</li>
    <li><strong>ease-in</strong> — Starts slow, ends fast</li>
    <li><strong>ease-out</strong> — Starts fast, ends slow</li>
    <li><strong>ease-in-out</strong> — Slow at both ends</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Performance Tips</h2>

  <p>For smooth 60fps animations, stick to:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>transform</strong> — translate, scale, rotate</li>
    <li><strong>opacity</strong> — fade in/out</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Avoid animating width, height, top, left, or margin. These trigger layout
    recalculations and will be janky on slower devices.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <div class="fp-nav-bar">
    <a href={`${base}articles/`} class="fp-nav-btn">◀ Back to Articles</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
